# =============================================================================
# DISA RHEL 9 STIG - rules Not Applicable to a CONTAINER image
# =============================================================================
# This file is the SOURCE for the tailoring ("answer") file. Every line that is
# not blank and not a comment is a rule id that generate-tailoring.sh feeds to
# `autotailor -u`, which marks it "notselected" so it is excluded from the
# compliance score. The comment block immediately ABOVE each rule id is the
# Not-Applicable justification, and it is copied verbatim into the generated
# XML so the assessor reads your reasoning inside the artifact itself.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE FOR ADDING SOMETHING TO THIS FILE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A rule belongs here when it is TECHNICALLY IMPOSSIBLE or MEANINGLESS to
# implement inside a container image, because the thing it controls belongs to
# the host, the node, or the orchestrator.
#
# A rule does NOT belong here because:
#   - it is annoying to fix
#   - the fix broke something and you did not investigate why
#   - you are behind schedule
#
# Those are RISK ACCEPTANCE decisions. They go through your AO as a waiver or a
# POA&M, not into this file. Deselecting a rule you simply did not fix hides it
# from the score and from your own dashboards - which is precisely the failure
# mode that makes people distrust compliance automation.
# See docs/12-waivers-and-poam.md.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YOU PROBABLY NEED FEWER ENTRIES THAN YOU THINK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenSCAP already resolves ~400 host-only rules to "notapplicable" on its own,
# using the CPE applicability logic baked into the SSG content: bootloader,
# disk partitioning, GUI/GNOME, kernel sysctls set at boot, auditd-as-a-service,
# FIPS kernel mode, and so on. Those never appear as FAIL, so they must NOT be
# listed here. Only list a rule that OpenSCAP scores as FAIL and that a human
# has determined is genuinely Not Applicable.
#
# Workflow: run the BASELINE scan first, read results/baseline/failed-rules.txt,
# and adjudicate each entry one at a time.
# =============================================================================


# --- ACTIVE: Not Applicable, would otherwise be scored FAIL -------------------

# DNS resolver configuration. Inside a container /etc/resolv.conf is generated
# and bind-mounted at runtime by the container engine or by the kubelet. Whatever
# the image ships is overwritten before the first process starts, so the setting
# cannot be enforced from the image. SSG ships no automated remediation for this
# rule. The control is satisfied at the node and cluster DNS layer.
xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_network_configure_name_resolution

# System-wide crypto policy set to FIPS:STIG. Applying it requires the kernel to
# be running in FIPS mode (fips-mode-setup --enable, which rewrites the
# bootloader and regenerates the initramfs, then reboots) plus the STIG crypto
# sub-policy module. A container has no kernel of its own - it uses the node's -
# so `update-crypto-policies --set FIPS:STIG` fails here with "FIPS mode is not
# enabled". FIPS is a node control that the container inherits; verify it on the
# node, not in the image.
xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_configure_crypto_policy


# --- TEMPLATE ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Uncomment an entry below only if a future SSG content release stops
# auto-detecting it as notapplicable and starts scoring it FAIL. Each one has
# its justification written already. Leaving them commented keeps the answer
# file honest: it lists only decisions that are actually load-bearing today.

# The bootloader belongs to the node. A container has no GRUB, no UEFI, and no
# boot process - it is a process tree in a namespace.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_grub2_password
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_grub2_uefi_password
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_grub2_audit_argument

# Filesystem layout and separate partitions are decided by the node's storage
# configuration. An image layer cannot create a mount point that the runtime
# will honour.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_partition_for_tmp
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_partition_for_var
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_partition_for_var_log_audit
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_partition_for_home

# The audit daemon runs on the node and captures syscalls for every container on
# it. Running auditd inside an unprivileged container is not possible (it needs
# CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL and a netlink socket the namespace does not provide) and
# would produce duplicate, unattributable records if it were.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_service_auditd_enabled
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_package_audit_installed

# The host firewall is not the container's concern. Network reachability is
# enforced by the orchestrator: NetworkPolicy, the service mesh, and the node's
# own firewall. See k8s/networkpolicy.yaml.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_service_firewalld_enabled
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_package_firewalld_installed

# Kernel tunables are global to the node. A sysctl set inside an unprivileged
# container either fails outright or, in the few namespaced cases, does not do
# what the rule intends. Set these on the node.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_sysctl_kernel_randomize_va_space
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_sysctl_kernel_dmesg_restrict
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_sysctl_net_ipv4_conf_all_accept_redirects

# Time synchronisation is a node service. Every container reads the node clock;
# running chronyd in a container is both unnecessary and unable to set the time.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_package_chrony_installed
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_service_chronyd_enabled

# A graphical desktop is never present in a server base image.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_package_xorg-x11-server-common_removed
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_dconf_gnome_screensaver_idle_delay

# AIDE performs file integrity monitoring against a baseline database. A
# container's root filesystem is immutable by design (readOnlyRootFilesystem,
# requirement 3.7) and the image digest already provides a stronger integrity
# guarantee than an in-container AIDE database that is rebuilt on every deploy.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_package_aide_installed
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_aide_build_database

# USB, Bluetooth, and other physical device controls: there is no physical
# hardware attached to a namespace.
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_kernel_module_usb-storage_disabled
#xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_kernel_module_bluetooth_disabled
